% Describe above table

As shown in table \ref{tab:samplecomp}, there is little difference between districts 
in and out of the sample for the year 2012 on a range of measures of school district 
characteristics. Politically, the sample and the non-respondents reflect the rest of 
the state of Wisconsin -- evenly split. The groups have similar levels of student 
economic disadvantage as measured by eligibility for free and reduced price lunch and 
similar proportions of minority students in their schools. 
Their communities have similar levels of income, owner occupied housing, and levels 
of education. Teacher salary and fringe benefit rates are also nearly identical, as 
is the teacher-pupil ratio.\footnote{2012 was selected as an exemplar year, but 
the pattern holds for all available years from 2007-2012, which comprise the focus 
of this study.} 

The biggest discrepancy is that sample districts tend to be larger on average than 
out-of-sample districts in terms of student membership. This is no surprise as the largest 
Wisconsin school districts publish their election results online, making them among 
the easiest to obtain. This makes my coverage of the largest five Wisconsin school 
districts perfect, while my coverage of the smallest Wisconsin school districts 
is lower. The sample districts tend to have slightly smaller account balances or 
district savings accounts on a per pupil basis which is likely related to their larger size. 
Finally, sample districts tend to have less property value per member, or taxable 
assets to raise revenue from, owing to the slightly higher populations of sample 
districts and the inclusion of all urban areas. All of these differences, however, 
are within one standard deviation of the mean from one another and do not represent 
a significant deviation either statistically or substantively. 
\clearpage
\subsection{The Bigs and the Smalls}

Wisconsin, like other states with independent school districts, has a great diversity 
in school district size, demographic make up, and structure. To illustrate the wide 
array of organizations that school boards in Wisconsin govern, I present Tables 
\ref{tab:small5} and \ref{tab:big5} describing the five smallest and five largest 
school districts in Wisconsin and how 
they compare on the variables above in Table \ref{tab:samplecomp}. These tables are intended 
to illustrate the range of organizational contexts that govern public K-12 education 
in Wisconsin and to illustrate the diversity of jurisdictions within which school 
board members are contesting elections. Table \ref{tab:small5} shows that the smallest 
school districts in Wisconsin have fewer than 100 students and fewer than 1,000 
residents. Smaller districts also tend to be much higher percentage Republican, 
less low-income, whiter, and have less disparity between the average teacher 
salary and the median income in the community. Contrast this with Table \ref{tab:big5} 
where the student population is 200 to 300 times larger than the smallest districts, 
the electorate is much more Democratic, student poverty is higher, and teacher 
salaries and median incomes are more divergent. 

