\subsection{General Elections, Primaries, and Special Elections}

General elections for school board members follow the schedule described in Section 
\ref{sec:wiscolaw}. However, for this project, I requested records for \emph{all} elections 
for school board office including school board member primaries and special elections. 
School board primaries occur when the number of candidates exceeds twice 
the number of seats open in a race.\footnote{In Wisconsin first class cities, 
only Milwaukee during the period of study, a primary must be held whenever there are more than two candidates for 
a race. Wis. Stat \S 8.11 (2m)} Special 
elections occur either if a vacancy occurs in a specific window or in a first class 
city, or if a candidate is recalled under Wis. Stat \S 9.10 (1) (a) 
which states that a petition for recall must be signed by electors equal 
in number to at least 25\% of the votes cast for the office of President of the United 
States at the last election within the same jurisdiction.\footnote{It is unclear if this 
would hold for school districts because the determination to hold the election is made by 
school district election official, who may determine that a reliable count of votes cast 
is not available and thus may defer to a formula specified in statute to determine the 
number of petitioners required to initiate a recall. Wis. Stat \S 9.10 (1) (c) to (d). 
Cited in a Staff Brief by the Joint Legislative Council Special Committee on Election 
Law Review, October 2004 \url{http://libcd.law.wisc.edu/~wilc/sb/sb\_2004\_07.pdf}} 
A special election is also required to fill a vacancy for board of school directors 
in the Milwaukee Public School system (MPS) [Wis. Stat \S 8.50 (intro.)]. A 
special election is not required to fill a vacancy for any other school district - 
instead vacancies are filled by appointment of the remaining members [Wis. Stat \S 17.26 (1)]. 


<<electype, results='asis'>>=
tabledf <- subset(races, year > 2006 & year < 2013)
tabledf$electype2 <- ifelse(tabledf$electiontype == 1, "General", 
                            ifelse(tabledf$electiontype == 2, "Primary", "Special"))
print(xtable(table(tabledf$year, tabledf$electype2), label="tab:electype", 
             caption = "Number of records for general, primary, and special school board elections 2007-2012."), 
      include.rownames=TRUE)
@

For the entire data set from 2002-2012 there are \Sexpr{iNum(length(races$distid[races$electiontype == 1]))} 
general election races,\Sexpr{iNum(length(races$distid[races$electiontype == 2]))} 
primary election races, and \Sexpr{iNum(length(races$distid[races$electiontype == 3]))}
special elections. Table \ref{tab:electype} shows the number of races of each type 
for the period between 2007 and 2012. In all, \Sexpr{iNum(length(unique(races$distid[races$electiontype == 2])))} districts 
reported a primary, with \Sexpr{iNum(length(unique(tabledf$distid[tabledf$electiontype == 2])))} 
reporting a primary between 2007 and 2012. Only \Sexpr{iNum(length(unique(tabledf$distid[tabledf$electiontype == 3])))}
district reported a special election during this period, and \Sexpr{iNum(length(unique(tabledf$distid[tabledf$electiontype == 1])))} 
reported a general election race. 

Since Wisconsin school boards are non-partisan offices, a primary is not a symbol of 
an ideological challenge to an incumbent or a battle to win the votes of 
strict partisans. Instead, a primary is a signal of a substantial level of candidate 
activity for a school board race. 

Only a single special election occurred during the 2007 to 2012 period. This was a
recall election held on December 29$^{th}$, 2009 in the Crivitz school district which 
was triggered after a petition against school board member David Kwiatkowski. Mr. Kwiatkowski 
resigned his seat that November in the face of the recall petition, but the recall 
election was still held. This special election raised several important issues that 
are indicative of the challenge surrounding the study of local elections. First, 
it was unclear how Wisconsin's recall statutes apply in the case of school boards. 
The recall statute allows for a recall based on the number of petitioners equal to 
at least 25\% of the vote cast for the office of Governor at the last gubernatorial 
election held within the same jurisdiction. \footnote{\url{http://gab.wi.gov/sites/default/files/publication/64/recall_of_local_elected_officials_manual_2009_06_p_13139.pdf}} As discussed above, Wisconsin does not 
have official records on the number of votes cast for each office by school district 
jurisdiction and in this case the threshold was calculated by the district administrator 
to be 668 - which is roughly the average number of voters who voted for school board. 
An additional difficulty was the overlap between many officials involved in the 
recall process in this small jurisdiction had overlapping responsibilities and allegations 
of conflicts of interest arose as a result -- leading to appeals to the Wisconsin 
Governmental Accountability Board.  
\footnote{For a full account of this 
particular case, see the Peshtigo Times article online \url{http://www.peshtigotimes.net/?id=12617}} 
In general, school board elections have fewer primary and special elections than 
their legislative counterparts. This leads to the biggest question in the school 
board literature from Chapter \ref{chap:intro}. 

\subsection{Contestation: Frequency and Context}

The biggest question in the school board literature seems to be -- are school 
board elections contested, and if so, when \citep{RefWorks:344, RefWorks:336, RefWorks:340}? 
While Chapter \ref{chap:candidacy} 
will explore when candidates emerge for school board elections, this section will 
provide a descriptive look at how often elections are contested, and how this varies compared to 
other elected bodies. 

Unlike studies of congressional or state legislative races, where the barriers to 
appearing on the ballot are generally understood, I begin by discussing the decisions 
a potential school board candidate faces when deciding to run. To appear on the 
ballot in the spring election a candidate may not begin circulating nomination 
papers prior to December 1 in the previous year. The nomination papers must be 
filed no later than 5 p.m. on the first Tuesday in January preceding the election. 
(Wis. Stat \S 8.10 and 8.15). The number of signatures by electors varies in statute, 
depending on the proximity of the school district to cities. In Milwaukee Public Schools 
candidates must obtain between 400 to 800 signatures within the jurisdiction they 
are running to represent (either across the school district, or a specific region 
of the district). 
If the school district contains any territory that lies within a second class city, a 
candidate must obtain between 100 and 200 signatures. School districts that have no 
territory that lies within a first or second class city are free to determine 
whether or not require nomination papers.\footnote{Milwaukee is the only first 
class city that meets all requirements in Wis. Stat \S 62.05 (I). There are currently 
sixteen second class cities in Wisconsin, and two more which qualify but have not switched 
status yet. The second class cities are Madison, Green Bay, Kenosha, Racine, Appleton, 
Waukesha, Oshkosh, Eau Claire, Janesville, West Allis, La Crosse, Sheboygan, Wauwatosa, 
Fond du Lac, Brookfield, and Superior.} [Wis. Stat \S 120.06 (a)] The statutes specify that these remaining 
districts can choose to require nomination papers from candidates, but may only require 
between 20 and 100 signatures of electors [Wis. Stat \S 8.10 (3) and 8.15 (6)]. In 
jurisdictions that do not require elector signatures, candidates must file a declaration 
of candidacy to register for the election with the election clerk in the school district.
After meeting these requirements the candidate will appear on the spring ballot as 
a candidate for school board. Outside of first and second class cities, running for school board presents very few barriers of entry for potential candidates. Even 
within first and second class cities, candidates face lower formal barriers than 
candidates for legislative or executive office. Coupled with the low cost of school board campaigns, most often fewer than \$1,000, the campaign presents 
very little friction toward serving on a school board \citep{RefWorks:322}. If we 
observe low levels of candidate participation in school board races, then, we must 
look to other explanations. 
%TODO: Explain why this is so and how the current study contributes to this

To explore candidate participation, let's consider the pool of school board races between 
2002 and 2012 for which records were successfully obtained. Of the \Sexpr{iNum(nrow(races))}, I focus on the \Sexpr{iNum(nrow(races[races$electiontype == 1,]))} 
general elections. Of these general election races, 
\Sexpr{iNum(nrow(races[races$electiontype == 1 & races$nrealcand > 1,]))} feature 
two or more candidates who either were on the ballot or garnered more than 10 votes.\footnote{
It is not uncommon in school board races for a write-in candidate to garner well over 10 votes. Thus, candidates not appearing on the ballot but receiving over 10 votes are included in the 
dataset. Candidates under 10 votes are collapsed into a category of scatter votes.} 
However, it is important to note that in many school districts voters select more than 
one candidate for each seat and elect the top N vote recipients to the board. A 
better measure of contestation is to see how many races featured more candidates than 
winners - \Sexpr{iNum(nrow(races[races$electiontype == 1 & races$nrealcand > races$nwins,]))} 
races featured this, or \Sexpr{iNum(nrow(races[races$electiontype == 1 & races$nrealcand > races$nwins,])/ 
nrow(races[races$electiontype == 1,])*100)}\% of all races. 

Next, let's look at how often incumbents stand for election and how often they are 
defeated. In order to code incumbency, two data sources were used. First, if a 
candidate appeared in consecutive races and was coded as a winner in the prior 
race, they are coded as an incumbent. However, for races that were the beginning 
of the dataset, incumbency was coded using a master roster of known school board 
members provided by the Wisconsin Department of Public Instruction to the author.\footnote{
This collection is not required or official, but is maintained by the DPI in partnership with 
the Wisconsin Association of School Boards.} This collection indicates name, school district, 
and year of service for school board members in Wisconsin. Candidates appearing on the ballot 
in the early years of a school district's election records were cross referenced against this 
list to infer their incumbency status. 

<<challeng1, echo=FALSE, results='hide'>>=
nrow(races[races$ninc > 0 & races$electiontype == 1,])
nrow(races[races$ninc > 1 & races$electiontype == 1,])
contested <- nrow(races[races$nrealcand > races$nwins & 
                          races$nwins > 0 & races$electiontype == 1, ])
contestedSer <- nrow(races[(races$nrealcand - races$nminor) > races$nwins & 
                          races$nwins > 0 & races$electiontype == 1 , ])
base <- nrow(races[races$electiontype == 1,])
@

% More candidates than seats

A basic requirement for voters to express a choice is to have multiple options to 
choose from. In order to assess this I first identify the number of races where 
there are more candidates on the ballot than winners. This is a nominal measure 
of the level of choice that voters are presented with. \Sexpr{iPer(contested/base*100)} 
of races feature at least one more candidate than the number of winners. However, 
while this represents the number of candidates appearing on the ballot, it does 
not provide an indication of the seriousness of the challenge. Merely having a 
multiple candidates is not the same as having a contested election. Political scientists 
focus on serious candidates and quality challengers. Measuring challenger quality 
in elections is difficult in any case, but particularly difficult in non-partisan 
off-cycle elections like school boards \citep{oliver12}. In particular in Wisconsin 
school board races, candidates may appear on the ballot but not garner even 20 votes. Other times, local election records did not distinguish between candidates who declared officially and any 
candidate who received votes. Thus, the \Sexpr{iper(contested/base*100)} figure 
is certainly an overestimate. In lieu of a better measure, I restrict challengers 
only to candidates that garner at least 20 votes -- which represents \Sexpr{iPer(20/median(races$votes[races$electiontype==1], na.rm=TRUE)*100)} of the votes cast in the median school board race. 
This represents arguably a conservative estimate, because in some cases a write-in candidate may garner 20 votes and in larger jurisdictions 20 votes are easier to come by than in smaller jurisdictions. Once I filter out all cases where a candidate received fewer than 20 votes, the level of contestation is \Sexpr{iper(contestedSer/base*100)} of general election races. 

% Challenger and incumbent
<<incumb1, echo=FALSE, results='hide'>>=
nrow(races[races$ninc > 0 & races$electiontype == 1,])
nrow(races[races$ninc > 1 & races$electiontype == 1,])
contested <- nrow(races[races$nrealcand > races$ninc & 
                          races$ninc > 0 & races$electiontype == 1, ])
contestedSer <- nrow(races[(races$nrealcand - races$nminor) > races$ninc & 
                          races$ninc > 0 & races$electiontype == 1 , ])
base <- nrow(races[races$electiontype == 1,])
@
Another important type of contestation is how often incumbents are challenged. Incumbent 
defeat is important to normative conceptions of the functioning of democracy. %TODO:citation 
In general elections, \Sexpr{iPer((nrow(races[races$ninc > 0 & races$electiontype == 1,])/base)*100)} of all races featured at least one incumbent. However, a more important figure is 
identifying how many races featured at least one challenger against an incumbent. 
\Sexpr{iPer(contested/base*100)} of Wisconsin school board general elections featured 
this pattern of contestation.  %TODO: Check citation here.
Only \Sexpr{iPer(contestedSer/base*100)} of races featured challengers that received more than 20 votes. \footnote{Some evidence to support the selection of 20 votes is that very few 
candidates that appeared on the ballot received fewer than 20 votes -- even in the 
smallest jurisdictions.} %TODO: Find evidence and count of this. 

Table \ref{tab:incumbentContest} shows the patterns of contestation in Wisconsin 
elections for the period from 2007-2012 for the approximately 400 races in each 
year. Two trends that appear are the decrease in uncontested incumbents running 
and contested open seats. Instead, there has been an increase in races featuring 
an open and uncontested seat. Chapter \ref{chap:candidacy} will investigate this 
more thoroughly.

<<incumbtable, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=

# Make a table showing races featuring incumbent challenger by year
races.tmp <- subset(races, electiontype ==1 & year > 2006 & year <2013)
races.tmp$contest <- "Uncontested"
races.tmp$contest[races.tmp$nrealcand == races.tmp$nwins & races.tmp$ninc > 0] <- "Open Uncontested"
races.tmp$contest[races.tmp$nrealcand == races.tmp$nwins & races.tmp$ninc == 0] <- "Incumbent Uncontested"
races.tmp$contest[races.tmp$nrealcand > races.tmp$nwins & races.tmp$ninc > 0] <- "Incumbent Contested"
races.tmp$contest[races.tmp$nrealcand > races.tmp$nwins & races.tmp$ninc ==0] <- "Open Contested"


table.tmp <- table(races.tmp$year, races.tmp$contest)
table.tmp <- prop.table(table.tmp, margin = 1) * 100
table.tmp <- apply(table.tmp, MARGIN = 2, FUN=iPer2)
row.names(table.tmp) <- 2007:2012
table.tmp <- xtable(t(table.tmp), label = "tab:incumbentContest", 
                    caption = "Races featuring a challenge to an incumbent 2007-2012.")

print(table.tmp, include.rownames=TRUE, size = "small")

@

% Comparisons

In comparison to school board races, legislative races at both the state and federal 
level have much higher rates of contestation. In 2012, 10 out of 435 U.S. House 
races were uncontested, and none of the 33 U.S. Senate races were uncontested. 
While these races are considerably more attractive to candidates due to the career and financial 
opportunities they represent, they are also more difficult to qualify for and 
labor intensive to compete in. State legislative races also exhibit high level 
of contestation. Taking Wisconsin as an example, in 2012 only 26 of 99 seats in the 
Wisconsin Assembly were uncontested, and only 8 out of 33 seats in the Wisconsin 
Senate were uncontested in 2012 and 2010 combined.\footnote{All results provided 
by Ballotpedia.} Another comparison would be to compare contestation of partisan 
primaries in legislative districts. While partisan offices often face at least 
nominal contestation and contestation of partisan seats can be explained by the 
competitiveness of the seat (based on redistricting), looking at the contestation 
levels of primaries...

%TODO: Compare primaries, how often are they contested vs. school board?

Candidates have many fewer reasons to run for school board. Despite the low barrier 
of entry, serving on a school board is much less prestigous than other offices, 
requires attending evening meetings, and in most cases offers only a modest stipend 
and no benefits \citep{RefWorks:321,RefWorks:322}. 

% Some more discussion here

\subsubsection{Close Races}

In Table \ref{tab:incumbentContest} we see that school board elections are contested more often than the literature from Chapter \ref{chap:intro} would lead us to believe. However, 
Table \ref{tab:incumbentContest} represents an overestimate of contestation by 
using a generous definition of a serious contest. Another way to look at this is 
to look at the differential vote share between winners and losers and count the 
number of close races that have occurred in Wisconsin.  

<<votemargin1, eval = FALSE>>=
cand.tmp <- dat[dat$candidateid!=99 & dat$electiontype==1, ]
votes.tmp <- merge(cand.tmp, as.data.frame(races)[races$nwins >0, c("raceid2", "votes", 
                                                                    "districtwide", "ninc",
                                                                    "nminor","nwins", "ncand",
                                                                    "nrealcand")], 
                   by = c("raceid2"), suffixes = c(".cand", ".race"))

votes.tmp$vote_share <- votes.tmp$votes.cand / votes.tmp$votes.race
votes.tmp$vote_share[is.na(votes.tmp$vote_share)] <- 0
plotdf <- as.data.table(votes.tmp)[, list(cand = length(winner), 
                                          winners = sum(winner),
                                          votescast = sum(votes.cand, na.rm=TRUE),
                                          winshare = sum(vote_share[winner ==1], na.rm=TRUE),
    winmargin = sum(vote_share[winner == 1], na.rm=TRUE) - sum(vote_share[winner == 0], na.rm=TRUE),
    winmargin2 = mean(vote_share[winner==1], na.rm=TRUE) - mean(vote_share[winner == 0]), na.rm=TRUE), 
                                   by = c("raceid2")]
plotdf$winmargin3 <- plotdf$winshare - (1/plotdf$cand * plotdf$winners)
plotdf$winmargin3[plotdf$cand == plotdf$winners] <- .5

errors <- subset(plotdf, winmargin < 0)
#plotdf <- reshape(plotdf, direction = "wide", timevar = "winner", idvar = c("distid", "year", "raceid"))

# hist(plotdf$winmargin3[plotdf$winners < plotdf$cand & !is.na(plotdf$votescast) & plotdf$votescast > 100])
@

For an analysis of the intensity of competitiveness in school board races, we turn 
our attention to races where voters have a choice -- those with serious candidates 
on the ballot. As we saw above, this is a much smaller subset of all races than 
the corresponding restriction would be for state or federal legislative offices. 
I restrict the sample to the 
\Sexpr{iPer(nrow(plotdf[plotdf$cand > plotdf$winners,])/nrow(plotdf) * 100)} of 
general election races that feature more candidates than winners. These races 
are most commonly for one, two, or three seats simultaneously. These multi-member 
districts pose a challenge in calculating the win margin and making comparisons 
across races with differing expected pluralities. 

%TODO: Table explaining the vote margin methods


\begin{table}[h!]
\begin{center}
\begin{small}
\caption{Methods of Calculating Vote Margin}
\label{tab:votemargin}
\begin{tabular}{| p{.15\textwidth} | p{.45\textwidth} | p{.225\textwidth}| p{.225\textwidth}|}
\hline
\textbf{Name}  & \textbf{Method} & \textbf{Pros} & \textbf{Cons} \\ \hline
Share by Each  & Sum of Vote Share of Winners - Sum of Vote Share of Losers & Clean & Biased by lopsided races \\ \hline
Two-Means & Mean of Winners, Mean of Losers vote share & Easy to calcualte & Biased by lopsided races   \\ \hline
Maximum Value & Calculate distance winners capture from maximum & Normalized & Not intuitive \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{small}
\end{center}
\end{table}

One method to do this is to take the share of votes won by each candidate as a start 
by taking votes for one candidate over the total votes cast in the election.\footnote{All 
school board elections in Wisconsin restrict voters to one vote per candidate -- 
though in multi-member districts voters may select two or three candidates total 
for election to office.} Then, the vote share for the winners is summed and the 
vote share for the losers is summed, and the difference is taken as the margin 
of victory for the winners. Another method takes the mean of the winners' vote 
share and the mean of the losers' vote share and differences these. A final measure 
captures the share of votes all the winners in the election captured above the 
maximum they can capture -- determined by the proportion of votes available to 
each candidate (1 over the number of candidates) multiplied by the number of winners. 
For a district with three candidates running for two seats, this would mean that 
the winners collectively need to win 66\% of the vote. If they win 70\% this equates 
to a margin of victory of 4\%. For a district with five candidates running for three seats, 
the winners collectively need to win 60\% of the vote. If they win 70\% of the vote 
this equates to a 10\% margin of victory, even though each candidate has only won 
on average 23.3\% of the vote in this race as compared to 35\% of the vote in the 
prior race. 

This final method has some limitations. First, school board race vote share measures 
are more heavily influenced by the presence of write-in candidates than state or 
federal legislative districts. This is because the average school board race has 
only \Sexpr{iNum(mean(plotdf$votescast, na.rm=TRUE))} votes cast and the median 
race has only \Sexpr{iNum(median(plotdf$votescast, na.rm=TRUE))}. A large number 
of scatter votes can thus swing the vote share calculation. Note that in the current 
data set, any candidate receiving more than 20 votes is counted as a candidate, but 
districts with many candidates receiving fewer than 20 votes will still be affected 
by this concern. This makes it possible for the winning candidates 
to collectively secure only 58\% of the votes and win election, resulting in a 
negative win margin from the expected total. 

%TODO: Find an example for the above

\subsubsection{Scattered Votes}

One adjustment to deal with this concern is to subtract out votes for write-in 
candidates where possible. This is not always possible because voting records do not 
always indicate in which school board race the scattered votes were cast. In most 
cases, school board election clerks record candidates getting very few votes -- 
less than 10 -- together into a single category of votes called "scatter". Though 
these votes are largely inconsequential in federal and state legislative races, 
in non-partisan local races with low vote counts they can make a significant 
impact on measures of turnout, win share, and contestation. Of the \Sexpr{iNum(nrow(dat))} 
candidate-races of data on school board elections in Wisconsin, \Sexpr{iNum(nrow(dat[dat$candidateid == 99, ]))} 
were scatter votes. 

Votes were labeled scatter in the Wisconsin data set for two reasons: first, all 
candidates receiving fewer than 10 votes in a single race were compiled together 
into a scatter category. This decision was put in place to reduce the data 
collection burden that already involved hand coding 
\Sexpr{iNum(nrow(dat[dat$candidateid != 99,]))} 
candidate-race records. Including the "long-tail" of write-in votes, protest votes, 
and spurious candidates would have added substantial burden to the data collection 
process. 

Second, some jurisdictions reported their votes with a "scatter" category for 
reporting in newspapers, to county election officials, 
or for local reporting. In these cases, the threshold for a candidate being labeled 
in the "scatter" category is unknown, however, for 
\Sexpr{iNum(nrow(dat[dat$candidateid == 99 & dat$votes > 20,]))} 
races it exceeds the threshold of 20 votes for label as a minor candidate, and for 
\Sexpr{iNum(nrow(dat[dat$candidateid == 99 & dat$votes > 200,]))} 
it exceeds 200. In these cases, it is important to consider how these votes should 
be included in further analysis -- should they be subtracted from the total votes 
in the district or should they be further collapsed into a category? 

%TODO: What should be done with these votes?

<<scatter1>>=
# scat1 <- dat[dat$candidateid == 99, ]
# head(scat1[order(-scat1$votes),], 20)
@

% \subsubsection{Write-in Candidates}
% 
% In only the rarest legislative elections are write-in candidates consequential with 
% only a handful of write-in candidates at the state or federal level over the past 
% 50 years making an impact at the polls. However, in school board elections in Wisconsin, 
% serious challenges and even victories by write-in candidates are frequent enough to 
% merit special consideration. Unfortunately, write-in candidates are often not 
% noted in election returns explicitly in Wisconsin school board elections. 

\subsection{Voter Turnout}
\label{subsec:turnout}

The spring elections in Wisconsin are decidedly low turnout affairs. Since 2000 
Wisconsin has had between 4 and 4.35 million voting age population residents. However, 
in this time period the spring election has seen around 800,000 votes cast on 
average with a peak of 1.5 million.\footnote{Statistics from a Wisconsin Government Accountability 
Board report, available online: \url{http://gab.wi.gov/sites/default/files/page/non\_partisan\_turnout\_2000\_2013\_pdf\_15069.pdf}}
Turnout is typically around 18\% in such elections. Unsurprisingly then, school 
board elections are also low turnout affairs. By comparison, 
in the November partisan general election voter turnout is around 70\% in the last 
three presidential elections, and around 48\% in the last three gubernatorial 
elections.\footnote{Statistics from a Wisconsin Government Accountability 
Board report, available online: \url{http://gab.wi.gov/sites/default/files/page/partisan\_voter_turnout\_statistics\_1948\_2012\_pdf\_20396.pdf}} \citet{teixeira92} suggests a 
rule of thumb that off-cycle partisan elections have a fifteen percentage point 
drop from the presidential election and non-partisan local elections experience a 
further fifteen point drop. This would mean Wisconsin should see 55\% turnout in 
gubernatiroal races and 40\% turnout in local elections. Numbers on typical school 
board election turnout are not available in the literature, so it is difficult to 
assess the degree Wisconsin's off-cycle races differ from other national races. 

A first concern with estimating turnout is finding the proper denominator. 
For the analysis here I elect to use Voting Age Population (VAP) as the measure for 
the denominator of the voter turnout calculation instead of registered or eligible 
voters. VAP is the only measure available both at the minor civil division (MCD) 
level and at the statewide level that can allow for consistent comparisons between the two. Furthermore, the state of Wisconsin does not maintain MCD level registered voter or eligible voter counts at the MCD level historically -- though such figures can be obtained from the GAB 
around election time for a current election.

Estimating school board election turnout is decidedly difficult because it requires 
both a reliable assessment of the votes cast in the election and a reliable 
estimate of the voting age population in the school district. Due to the fact that 
school districts have diverse organizational structure, and no requirements 
on election reporting to a central authority, it is often difficult to ascertain 
the number of votes cast by voters within the school district. To determine the VAP 
of the school district in Wisconsin, I employ the finest resolution 
estimate of voting age population available -- VAP estimates by minor civil division (MCD) 
produced by the Wisconsin Department of Administration.\footnote{Available online: \url{http://gab.wi.gov/elections-voting/results}. 
Statistics for Wisconsin Minor Civil Divisions are maintained by the Department of Administration 
Demographic Services Center: \url{http://www.doa.state.wi.us/section\_detail.asp?linkcatid=11\&linkid=64\&locid=9}}
However, MCDs are not perfectly contiguous with school districts -- school districts 
are contiguous only at the ward level where VAP estimates are not available. In 
order to estimate the proportion of each MCD's VAP that belongs to a given school district, 
I use property tax records provided by the Wisconsin Department of Revenue (DOR). 
These records annually report for each school district every MCD that is located entirely or partially within the boundaries of the school district, as well as the share of property wealth 
(as measured by equalized values, or EQV), for that MCD that the school district 
is authorized to levy tax for. For each year, the share of every MCD's EQV that 
was allocated to each school district was calculated. I use this share to 
prorate the population of each school district both for the purposes of 
calculating the voting age population, and for calculating votes cast for and 
against candidates in statewide elections. An additional adjustment 
is made to interpolate the VAP for school districts to correct for adjustments in 
the creation and destruction of MCDs year to year which can influence the VAP 
estimates for a school district.\footnote{A sensitivity check was done 
to determine if weighting by residential property values only changed the estimates, but 
it did not. For more details see Appendix \ref{app:data}}

We can measure how accurate this estimation procedure is by comparing the official number 
of votes cast and voter turnout statewide to the statewide estimates derived by 
rebuilding voter data at the district level. 

<<turnout, echo = FALSE,  results='asis'>>=
# qplot(races.tmp$turnout, races.tmp$topPresTurnoutPrior, geom = 'point') + 
#   ylim(c(0,1)) + xlim(c(0,1)) + geom_smooth()

years <- c(2002, 2004, 2006, 2008, 2010, 2012)
turnoutEst <- merge(VAP_dist, presTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)
turnoutEst <- merge(turnoutEst, govTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)

turnoutOf <- c(1785710, 3016288, 2166671, 2996869, 2171331, 3071434)
vapOf <- c(4060973, 4118621, 4260038, 4330695, 4372347, 4378741)
turnoutPerOf <- c(44, 72.9, 50.9, 69.2, 49.7, 70.1)

vapEst <- c(sum(turnoutEst$VAP[turnoutEst$year == 2002], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP[turnoutEst$year == 2004], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP[turnoutEst$year == 2006], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP[turnoutEst$year == 2008], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP[turnoutEst$year == 2010], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP[turnoutEst$year == 2012], na.rm =TRUE))


vapadjEst <- c(sum(turnoutEst$VAP_adj[turnoutEst$year == 2002], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP_adj[turnoutEst$year == 2004], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP_adj[turnoutEst$year == 2006], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP_adj[turnoutEst$year == 2008], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP_adj[turnoutEst$year == 2010], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$VAP_adj[turnoutEst$year == 2012], na.rm =TRUE))


turnoutEst <- c(sum(turnoutEst$topGovVotesPrior[turnoutEst$year == 2002], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$topPresVotesPrior[turnoutEst$year == 2004], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$topGovVotesPrior[turnoutEst$year == 2006], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$topPresVotesPrior[turnoutEst$year == 2008], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$topGovVotesPrior[turnoutEst$year == 2010], na.rm =TRUE), 
            sum(turnoutEst$topPresVotesPrior[turnoutEst$year == 2012], na.rm =TRUE))

turnoutPerEst <- (turnoutEst / vapadjEst)*100

tab1names <- c("Year", "Official Total Votes", "Offical VAP", "Offical Turnout", 
               "Estimated Total Votes", "Estimated VAP", "Estimated Turnout")
table1 <- cbind(years, turnoutOf, vapOf, turnoutPerOf, turnoutEst, vapEst, 
                turnoutPerEst)
table1 <- as.data.frame(table1)
table1$years <- as.character(table1$years)
names(table1) <- tab1names
rm(years, turnoutOf, vapOf, turnoutPerOf, turnoutEst, vapEst, turnoutPerEst, 
   tab1names, vapadjEst)


table1 <- xtable(table1, digits =0, label="tab:turnoutEst", 
             caption = "Comparing estimates of voter turnout derived from MCD estimates to official GAB turnout records." )
align(table1) <- c('p{.3in}','p{.3in}',  'p{.4in}','p{.4in}','p{.5in}','p{.5in}',
                   'p{.4in}','p{.4in}')

print(table1, 
      include.rownames = FALSE, 
      format.args = list(big.mark = ",", decimal.mark = "."), 
      size = "footnotesize")



@

As shown in Table \ref{tab:turnoutEst}, the estimation procedure accurately 
reproduces the statewide official turnout estimates. Using these estimates, 
we can be confident that the procedure used generates 
reasonable estimates of the VAP at the school district level. This takes care 
of the denominator of the turnout equation. 

The numerator, the number of voters casting votes for school board presents a 
second set of challenges. 
I begin by collecting election returns from as many school districts as possible 
in Wisconsin.\footnote{For details on the collection procedure see Appendix \ref{app:data}} 
However, unlike a top ticket race such as a presidential or gubernatorial race, 
the election rules vary across school district jurisdictions. In some districts, 
voters may have multiple votes to cast in the election -- eliminating the ability 
to judge voter turnout based on the number of votes cast. In other districts, only 
a subsection of the electorate may be eligible to vote for a school district office 
due to apportionment within the district. 

<<turnoutgraph, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Turnout for district wide school board races.">>=
races <- as.data.frame(races)
races.tmp <- merge(races, VAP_dist, by = c("distid", "year"))

presTurn$presTwoPartyVote <- presTurn$presDemVotes + presTurn$presRepVotes
presTurn$presTwoPartyShareDem <- presTurn$presDemVotes / presTurn$presTwoPartyVote

govTurn$govTwoPartyVote <- govTurn$govDemVotes + govTurn$govRepVotes
govTurn$govTwoPartyShareDem <- govTurn$govDemVotes / govTurn$govTwoPartyVote

# turnout over the prior presidential election
races.tmp <- merge(races.tmp, presTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)
races.tmp <- merge(races.tmp, govTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, select = c("distid", "year", "raceid2", "ncand", 
                                          "nrealcand", "nwins", "ninc", "raceid",
                                          "nminor", "votes", "districtwide", 
                                          "electiontype", "VAP", "VAP_adj", 
                                          "topPresVotesPrior", "presTwoPartyShareDem", 
                                          "topGovVotesPrior", "govTwoPartyShareDem"))

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, raceid!=0)
# look at general
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, electiontype == 1)

# Look at districtwide only
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, districtwide == 1)
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, nrealcand > 0)

races.tmp <- races.tmp[!is.na(races.tmp$distid),]
races.tmp$voters <- races.tmp$votes / races.tmp$nwins
races.tmp$turnout <- races.tmp$voters/races.tmp$VAP_adj
qplot(year, turnout, data = races.tmp, geom = 'boxplot') + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) + theme_dpi()
@

There is one set of cases for which estimating voter turnout is easy - the case 
where a school board seat is chosen district wide. As shown in Table \ref{tab:yearsample}, 
this is the vast majority of Wisconsin school districts. To determine if a seat 
was apportioned or district wide, I coded the seats for labels such as "Area", "District", 
"Region", or "Village" as opposed to "At-Large". As district wide is the default in state law, 
in the absence of evidence, I assume the race is district wide.  Figure \ref{fig:turnoutgraph} 
shows that district wide school board races feature an average turnout below 25\%. 

<<comparison, results='asis', echo=FALSE>>=
out1 <- ddply(races.tmp, .(year), summarize, 
              nraces = length(distid), 
              ndists = length(unique(distid)))
names(out1) <- c("year", "nDistWideRaces", "nDistrictsDistWide")

out2 <- ddply(races, .(year), summarize, 
              nraces = length(distid), 
              ndists = length(unique(distid)))
names(out2) <- c("year", "nAllRaces", "nAllDistricts")

table2 <- merge(out1, out2)
rm(out1, out2)
table2$difference <- table2[, 5] - table2[, 3]
table2$difference2 <- table2[, 4] - table2[, 2]
names(table2) <- c("Year", "District Wide Races", "Districts with District Wide", 
                   "All SB Races", "All Districts with Records", 
                   "Districts without District Wide", "Non-district Wide Races")


table2 <- xtable(table2, label="tab:yearsample", 
             caption = "Comparing sample size for district wide vs. apportioned school board races." )
align(table2) <- c('p{.2in}','p{.5in}',  'p{1in}','p{1in}','p{.75in}','p{.75in}',
                   'p{.75in}','p{.75in}')

print(table2, 
      include.rownames = FALSE, 
      format.args = list(big.mark = ",", decimal.mark = "."), 
      size = "footnotesize")

@

Estimating turnout in the roughly 50 districts annually, as shown in Table \ref{tab:yearsample}, which do not have a district wide school board race is a different matter. 

<<nondistraces, fig.cap="Turnout for non-district wide school board races.">>=
races <- as.data.frame(races)
races.tmp <- merge(races, VAP_dist, by = c("distid", "year"))

presTurn$presTwoPartyVote <- presTurn$presDemVotes + presTurn$presRepVotes
presTurn$presTwoPartyShareDem <- presTurn$presDemVotes / presTurn$presTwoPartyVote

govTurn$govTwoPartyVote <- govTurn$govDemVotes + govTurn$govRepVotes
govTurn$govTwoPartyShareDem <- govTurn$govDemVotes / govTurn$govTwoPartyVote

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, select = c("distid", "year", "raceid2", "ncand", 
                                          "nrealcand", "nwins", "ninc", "raceid",
                                          "nminor", "votes", "districtwide", 
                                          "electiontype", "VAP_adj"))

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, raceid!=0)
# look at general
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, electiontype == 1)
# Look at non-districtwide only
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, districtwide == 0)
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, nrealcand > 0)
races.tmp <- races.tmp[!is.na(races.tmp$distid),]

# collapse to the district level

dist.tmp <- ddply(races.tmp, .(distid, year), summarize, 
              ncand = sum(ncand), nrealcand = sum(nrealcand), 
              nwins = sum(nwins), ninc = sum(ninc), 
              nminor = sum(nminor), votes = sum(votes), 
              VAP_adj = statamode(VAP_adj), 
              nraces = length(distid))

# turnout over the prior presidential election
dist.tmp <- merge(dist.tmp, presTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)
dist.tmp <- merge(dist.tmp, govTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)
dist.tmp$VAP_adj <- as.numeric(dist.tmp$VAP_adj)

dist.tmp$voters <- dist.tmp$votes / dist.tmp$nwins
dist.tmp$turnout <- dist.tmp$voters / dist.tmp$VAP_adj
qplot(year, turnout, data = dist.tmp, geom = 'boxplot') + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) + theme_dpi()

@

Figure \ref{fig:nondistraces} shows the pattern for non-district wide races appears 
very similar under the estimation method of collapsing all races in a district 
into one race and dividing the votes counted by the number of winners. This 
represents an underestimate, as even districts with apportioned seats 
do not elect all portions of their district in the same election cycle or even 
have consistent apportionment from one cycle to the next.

%TODO: Explain this better.

Putting these two together, we can estimate overall voter turnout in school board 
races by school district, over time. To do this, we prefer the district wide race 
turnout. If no district wide race is present in a given district in a given year, 
we then use the adjusted districtwide turnout based on the apportioned seats 
available. 

<<overallturnoutest, fig.cap="Turnout for school board races by school district.">>=
races <- as.data.frame(races)
races.tmp <- merge(races, VAP_dist, by = c("distid", "year"))

# turnout over the prior presidential election
races.tmp <- merge(races.tmp, presTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)
races.tmp <- merge(races.tmp, govTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, select = c("distid", "year", "raceid2", "ncand", 
                                          "nrealcand", "nwins", "ninc", "raceid",
                                          "nminor", "votes", "districtwide", 
                                          "electiontype", "VAP", "VAP_adj", 
                                          "topPresVotesPrior", "presTwoPartyShareDem", 
                                          "topGovVotesPrior", "govTwoPartyShareDem"))

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, raceid!=0)
# look at general
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, electiontype == 1)

# Look at districtwide only
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, districtwide == 1)
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, nrealcand > 0)

races.tmp <- races.tmp[!is.na(races.tmp$distid),]
races.tmp$voters <- races.tmp$votes / races.tmp$nwins
races.tmp$turnout <- races.tmp$voters/races.tmp$VAP_adj
## Convert to district average somehow

dist_turn <- ddply(races.tmp, .(distid, year), summarize, 
              ncand = sum(ncand), nrealcand = sum(nrealcand), 
              nwins = sum(nwins), ninc = sum(ninc), 
              nminor = sum(nminor), votes = sum(votes), 
              VAP_adj = statamode(VAP_adj), 
              nraces = length(distid))

dist_turn$districtwide <- 1
############
# Non district-wide
############
races <- as.data.frame(races)
races.tmp <- merge(races, VAP_dist, by = c("distid", "year"))

presTurn$presTwoPartyVote <- presTurn$presDemVotes + presTurn$presRepVotes
presTurn$presTwoPartyShareDem <- presTurn$presDemVotes / presTurn$presTwoPartyVote

govTurn$govTwoPartyVote <- govTurn$govDemVotes + govTurn$govRepVotes
govTurn$govTwoPartyShareDem <- govTurn$govDemVotes / govTurn$govTwoPartyVote

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, select = c("distid", "year", "raceid2", "ncand", 
                                          "nrealcand", "nwins", "ninc", "raceid",
                                          "nminor", "votes", "districtwide", 
                                          "electiontype", "VAP_adj"))

races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, raceid!=0)
# look at general
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, electiontype == 1)
# Look at non-districtwide only
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, districtwide == 0)
races.tmp <- subset(races.tmp, nrealcand > 0)
races.tmp <- races.tmp[!is.na(races.tmp$distid),]

# collapse to the district level

dist.tmp <- ddply(races.tmp, .(distid, year), summarize, 
              ncand = sum(ncand), nrealcand = sum(nrealcand), 
              nwins = sum(nwins), ninc = sum(ninc), 
              nminor = sum(nminor), votes = sum(votes), 
              VAP_adj = statamode(VAP_adj), 
              nraces = length(distid))

dist.tmp$districtwide <- 0

dist_turn <- rbind(dist_turn, dist.tmp)

row_counts <- ddply(dist_turn, .(distid, year), nrow)
dist_turn <- merge(dist_turn, row_counts, all.x=TRUE)
dist_turn$recs <- dist_turn$V1; dist_turn$V1 <- NULL
dist_turn1 <- dist_turn[dist_turn$recs >= 1 & dist_turn$districtwide > 0,]
dist_turn2 <- dist_turn[dist_turn$recs == 1 & dist_turn$districtwide == 0,]

dist_turn <- rbind(dist_turn1, dist_turn2)
dist_turn$recs <- NULL

## Clean up metrics
# turnout over the prior presidential election
dist_turn <- merge(dist_turn, presTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)
dist_turn <- merge(dist_turn, govTurn, by = c("distid", "year"), all.x=TRUE)
dist_turn$VAP_adj <- as.numeric(dist_turn$VAP_adj)
dist_turn$voters <- dist_turn$votes / dist_turn$nwins
dist_turn$turnout <- dist_turn$voters / dist_turn$VAP_adj

qplot(year, turnout, data = dist_turn[dist_turn$turnout < 0.75 & !is.na(dist_turn$turnout),], geom = 'boxplot') + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) + theme_dpi() + 
  labs(x = "Year", y = "Turnout as Share of VAP Estimate")
@

Figure \ref{fig:overallturnoutest} shows the final estimate of the distribution 
of school board voter turnout among reporting districts across the years of data 
available. Both the median and the distribution of turnout by school district 
remains consistent from 2002-2010. However, 2011 and 2012 exhibit a sharp spike 
upward in voter turnout. These elections represent the first two spring elections 
after the passage of Act 10 and the election of Governor Scott Walker. 

\clearpage

\subsection{Rolloff}
\label{subsec:undervote}

It is difficult to draw conclusions about school board voter turnout separate 
from the lower turnout more generally associated with non-partisan offices and 
Wisconsin's spring non-partisan election specifically. One way to approach this 
is to look at the rolloff for school board office relative to the top ticket 
race in those spring elections. For the 2007-2012 period, I estimate the top ticket 
turnout in each school district. Importantly, in this period, several different 
office types were the top ticket due to the pattern of statewide office terms 
in Wisconsin. These consist of primarily state supreme court races, but as there 
seven justices elected to ten-year terms, the state supreme court does not have 
an election every spring: 

\begin{myitemize}
  \item 2007 - State Supreme Court Race
  \item 2008 - State Supreme Court Race * (alternative presidential primary)
  \item 2009 - State Superintendent of Public Instruction
  \item 2010 - None. Wisconsin Appeals Courts (3 of 4)
  \item 2011 - State Supreme Court Race
  \item 2012 - Presidential Preference Primary
\end{myitemize}


<<undervoting, fig.cap="Spring Turnout For Top Ticket and School Board Compared">>=
load("../../data/cache/springElectionVotes.rda")
# load("data/cache/springElectionVotes.rda")
springVotes$votesTopTicketSpring <- springVotes$VotesCast
springVotes$VotesCast <- NULL
dist_turn <- merge(dist_turn, springVotes, 
                   by = c("distid", "year"), all.x = TRUE)

dist_turn$topTicketTurnoutSpring <- dist_turn$votesTopTicketSpring / dist_turn$VAP_adj

springVotes.tmp <- springVotes[springVotes$year == 2020, ]
springVotes.tmp$year <- 2010
dist_turn.tmp <- merge(dist_turn[, 1:25], springVotes.tmp, 
                   by = c("distid", "year"))
dist_turn.tmp$topTicketTurnoutSpring <- dist_turn.tmp$votesTopTicketSpring / dist_turn.tmp$VAP_adj

dist_turn <- rbind(dist_turn, dist_turn.tmp)
dist_turn <- dist_turn[!duplicated(dist_turn), ]
rm(dist_turn.tmp, springVotes.tmp)

plotdat <- subset(dist_turn, select = c("turnout", "topTicketTurnoutSpring", 
                                        "ImputedFlag", "year", "distid"))
plotdat$ImputedFlag[is.na(plotdat$ImputedFlag)] <- "Observed"
plotdat <- na.omit(plotdat)
centr <- mean(plotdat$turnout)
stddev <- sd(plotdat$turnout)
plotdat$outflag <- (plotdat$turnout - centr) / stddev
plotdat <- plotdat[plotdat$outflag <= 5,]

# qplot(topTicketTurnoutSpring, turnout, data = plotdat, shape = ImputedFlag, 
#       color = factor(year)) + 
#   geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = 2) + theme_dpi()
# 

plotdat$ImputedFlag <- defac(plotdat$ImputedFlag)
plotdat$ImputedFlag[plotdat$ImputedFlag == "Imputed2010"] <- "Imputed"
plotdat$ImputedFlag[plotdat$ImputedFlag == "Observed2010"] <- "Observed"
plotdat$ImputedFlag <- factor(plotdat$ImputedFlag, levels = c("Imputed", "Observed"))
plotdat$ImputedFlag <- relevel(plotdat$ImputedFlag, ref = "Imputed")
# levels(plotdat$ImputedFlag) <- c("Observed", "Imputed")
plotdat <- plotdat[!duplicated(plotdat[, c(4, 5)]),]

mypal <- c("Imputed" = "red", "Observed" = "black")

qplot(topTicketTurnoutSpring, turnout, data = plotdat, color = ImputedFlag) + 
  geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = 2) + theme_dpi() + 
  scale_color_manual(name = "Imputed", values = mypal) + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + scale_y_continuous("School Board Turnout", label = percent) + 
  scale_x_continuous("Top Ticket Turnout", label = percent) + 
  stat_smooth(method = 'rlm', aes(group = 1), se = FALSE, size = 1.1, color = I("blue")) 

@

If top ticket races drive voter turnout, then examining the degree to which school 
board turnout leads or lags top ticket races may provide some insight into the 
political activity in school board races independent of such races. Voter rolloff 
after the top ticket arises for a number of reasons including ballot design, 
lack of information, and the complexity of choices facing the voter in a given 
election \citep{wattenberg00}. In the non-partisan context of judicial elections, 
there is evidence that rolloff is lessened in competitive races where information 
is increased \citep{streb09}. 

To do this, I apply the same method described in Section \ref{subsec:turnout} to 
estimate school district level voter turnout, instead using records from spring 
election MCD votes. This method poses a few different complications when applied 
to the spring election. First, spring election records provided by the Wisconsin 
GAB use inconsistent reporting units depending on the top ticket of the race. This 
means that first the reporting units must be aggregated into MCDs correctly, before 
MCDs can be assigned to school districts. Second, in the spring of 2010 there was no 
statewide top ticket -- instead 
\Sexpr{iPer((length(springVotes$ImputedFlag[springVotes$ImputedFlag=="Observed2010" & springVotes$year == 2010])/length(springVotes$year[springVotes$year==2010]))*100)} of jurisdictions 
had a top ticket race of Wisconsin Circuit Court judges. The other jurisdictions 
had only local elections on the ballot. To avoid losing these observations, I impute 
the 2010 estimated turnout as if the top ticket was a state supreme court race or 
election for state superintendent.\footnote{Imputation was done using model averaging 
of three k-nearest-neighbor models using votes cast in other spring elections as the 
primary predictors.} I expect the majority of Wisconsin districts to exhibit an 
undervote in school board elections relative to these top ticket races. 

Figure \ref{fig:undervoting} shows the relationship between top ticket turnout 
and school board turnout. 

%TODO: citation for topticket races

<<undervotetab, results='asis', echo=FALSE>>=
plotdat$vote_diff <- plotdat$topTicketTurnoutSpring - plotdat$turnout
# 
# length(plotdat$vote_diff[plotdat$vote_diff < 0]) / nrow(plotdat)
# length(plotdat$vote_diff[plotdat$vote_diff > 0]) / nrow(plotdat)

yearStats <- ddply(plotdat, .(year, ImputedFlag), summarize, 
                   overvote = length(vote_diff[vote_diff < 0]), 
                   undervote = length(vote_diff[vote_diff > 0]), 
                   sbturnout = mean(turnout), 
                   springturn = mean(topTicketTurnoutSpring))
yearStats$undervote_per <- yearStats[, 4] / (yearStats[, 3] + yearStats[, 4])
yearStats$overvote_per <- 1 - yearStats$undervote_per


names(yearStats) <- c("Year", "Impute?", "Districts Overvoting", "Districts Undervoting", 
                   "Avg.SB Turnout", "Avg.Top Turnout", 
                   "% Districts Undervote", "% Districts Overvote")
yearStats[, 5] <- iPer2(yearStats[,5]*100)
yearStats[, 6] <- iPer2(yearStats[,6]*100)
yearStats[, 7] <- iPer2(yearStats[,7]*100)
yearStats[, 8] <- iPer2(yearStats[,8]*100)


yearStats <- xtable(yearStats, label="tab:undervotestats", 
             caption = "Overvote and Undervote in Spring Elections." )
align(yearStats) <- c('p{.15in}','p{.35in}','p{.2in}', 'p{.3in}','p{.4in}','p{.4in}','p{.4in}',
                   'p{.4in}','p{.4in}')

print(yearStats, 
      include.rownames = FALSE, 
      format.args = list(big.mark = ",", decimal.mark = "."), 
      size = "footnotesize")

@



\clearpage

\section{Conclusion}

Wisconsin is a valuable case to study in the examination of school board elections. 
The statewide nature of this data collection allows for the exploration of the level 
of contestation and turnout in school board elections across a variety of contexts and 
within the same state political system. 

From the data collection we can already see that school boards are less frequently 
contested than many higher offices -- confirming prior work. School boards, despite 
their lower barrier to ballot entry and campaign costs, are contested less than half 
the time. School board elections are less prestigious, carry little financial benefit 
for members, and often require late evening meetings -- making them less attractive 
to potential candidates. School boards are also not shown to be a stepping stone 
to further political office, reducing their attractiveness to potential political 
candidates. 

Chapter 3 will seek to explore further the predictors of candidates 
emerging for office by exploring what local and state level factors may explain the 
emergence of a challenger to a school board election. Theory suggests that events 
like failed school bond referenda, turnover in district leadership, or fiscal pressure 
may drive local challenges. Theory also suggests that changing the powers of the school board, 
partisan identification of school board candidates, or political polarization may 
drive school board challenges to emerge statewide. Evidence of these state and 
local factors will be explored and evaluated to test whether theories of candidate 
emergence explain the case of school boards. 

Voters vote less in school board elections as well. In Wisconsin school board elections 
are held during the non-partisan spring elections, which have much lower turnout 
than partisan fall elections. In general, school board voter turnout hovers around 
20\% in school districts, but has plenty of variability. Voter turnout should be 
suppressed in school board elections because of both their non-partisan nature and 
their lower probability of being contested. School board researchers have long evaluated 
the dissatisfaction theory, which suggests that school boards exhibit higher contestation 
and voter turnout when local policy conditions change and the electorate is unsatisfied 
with the governance of the board. 

Chapter 4 will futher explore the puzzle of low school board turnout and identify 
if voter turnout does indeed respond to policy shocks. Local policy shocks such 
as leadership turnover and school fiscal challenges will be evaluated as well as 
statewide policy shocks like the passage of Act 10 and budget cuts. This will 
represent the fullest test of dissatisfaction theory to date and the first time 
exogenous policy shocks are leveraged to explore the causal relationship between 
changes in policy and voter turnout. 